Supporting inclusion as a leader goes beyond just inviting diverse voices to the table. It requires actively promoting a culture that enables
people to feel appreciated, valued, safe, respected, engaged and able to be authentic. This in turn facilitates their ability and willingness to contrib
ute their best to their work, leading to organizational success (Ferdman et al., 2021). Here we focus on going beyond “doing no harm” and advo
cate for practices team leaders can use to increase diversity, equity and inclusion on statistics and data science consulting teams. These “doing
good” practices generally fall under the umbrella of established best practices for management and leadership (Morgan et al., 2022). Unfortu
nately, most data science or statistics consulting team members in academic institutions, from students to centre directors, receive very limited
leadership and management training (Leiserson & McVinney, 2015). The lack of such fundamental practices compromises diversity, equity and
inclusion initiatives at all levels because they have no foundation on which to build and limited accountability.
There are many ways team leaders can support an inclusive environment, from flexible work arrangements to creating mechanisms for
employee feedback. This paper highlights nine leadership and management practices that are especially relevant to the dynamics of data science
or statistics consulting teams in an academic environment: ensuring people get credit, making tacit knowledge explicit, establishing clear perfor
mance review processes, championing career development, empowering team members to work autonomously, learning from diverse experi
ences, supporting team members in navigating power dynamics, having difficult conversations and developing foundational management skills.
Active engagement in these areas will help those who lead data science or statistics consulting groups – whether faculty or staff, regardless of
title – create and support inclusive teams.
2 | WHO THIS IS FOR
While all team members need to contribute to building an inclusive environment, and leadership can come from people at all levels of an organiza
tion, the focus here is on team supervisors, managers or directors (“team leaders”). Whether they have formal managerial responsibilities, team
leaders are those who have contact with all team members, influence employment decisions, set policies, establish common practices, undertake
performance evaluations and are positioned and empowered to implement the practices discussed here.
To be successful, team leaders need support from not only their team but from their own supervisors and institution executives as well.
Those who support team leaders as their supervisors, department chairs, directors or via other senior positions can also implement many of these
practices. Going a step further, they can directly acknowledge, promote the impact of and reward this type of work by the team leaders they
oversee.
While much of the focus of the literature discussing academic statistics and data science professionals has been on those in faculty-based
positions, predominantly ones on the tenure track (Board of Directors of the American Statistical Association, 2018; Mazumdar et al., 2015;
Utts & Goretsky, 2021; Waller, 2018), this manuscript is inclusive of all statistics and data sciences consulting teams in academic environments,
and potentially in some industrial or governmental environments as well. These are groups that provide statistics and data science services or
resources along all parts of the research enterprise, including study design; data collection, management, storage, visualization, analysis; interpre
tation of results; and grant support. Such groups vary in the research areas they support (e.g. oncology, business informatics, visualization), the
units in which they reside (e.g. library, IT, research core, academic department) and the types of positions occupied by team members
(e.g. student, staff, tenure-track faculty, research faculty). While faculty in academic departments may engage in some statistics and data science
consulting, the teams described in this manuscript explicitly coordinate their activities, share common leadership and self-identify as a team
or unit.
3 | ENSURE PEOPLE GET CREDIT
Ensuring that team members receive appropriate credit for their work will support future advancement opportunities
Everyone deserves acknowledgement for their efforts, and it should be an intuitive and straightforward practice. Yet often women, people
from marginalized communities, academic staff, students and individuals unfamiliar with merit and acknowledgement practices may find them
selves left out without recognition of their efforts and contributions (Liu et al., 2023; Ross et al., 2022). Some cultural differences may exist as well
in who gets credit and who does not. Some cultures, for example, may “emphasize social connectedness, interdependence, and in-group goals;
while [other cultures may]...emphasize self-reliance, independence, and personal goals” (Cortina et al., 2017). In settings where there is an empha
sis on team-based outcomes, team members who are focused on personal goals may have a more difficult time receiving credit than those with
in-group goals. Without sufficient credit for efforts, individuals have fewer advancement opportunities, experience low morale and a reduced
sense of belonging and may ultimately move on from their position, risking the health of the entire team (Castrillon, 2022; White, 2014). When
team members receive credit for their contributions, it not only validates their efforts but also promotes a sense of belonging and equality within
the team. This recognition sends a powerful message that every member's unique skills and perspectives are valued, regardless of their back
ground or position within the organization.
