In academia, the primary currency of achievement and advancement is authorship (see Kowalczyk et al., 2022, for example). Yet academic col
laborative data scientists and statisticians often have to “be assertive with respect to joint authorship” – something that may be more difficult for
those who have been discouraged from such behaviours in the past (Griffith et al., 2022). Therefore, it is important that team leaders support their
team members through authorship discussions. Having written policies to which they can refer may set expectations and prevent uncomfortable
situations. For example, team leaders can ensure their team members have knowledge of and practice using the broadly relevant International
Committee of Medical Journal Editors (ICMJE; n.d.) criteria for defining authorship and contributor roles and the Contributor Roles Taxonomy (n.
d.) in discussions about authorship. The ICMJE recommendations include as one sufficient criteria for authorship that an individual provides “sub
stantial contributions to the conception or design of the work; or the acquisition, analysis, or interpretation of data for the work.” The data science
or statistical collaborator often exceeds this criteria by being involved in at least the analysis and interpretation of results. Cooke et al. (2021) pro
vide useful strategies for preventing and navigating authorship disputes that may occur.
While authorship order, frequency of citations and journal impact within the field can play a role in an academician's advancement (see
Walker et al., 2010, for example), collaborative statisticians and data scientists should not be expected by their management or position evaluation
criteria to be a first, second or corresponding author or publish exclusively in statistics or data science-centric journals (Board of Directors of the
American Statistical Association, 2018). Team leaders should applaud successful collaborations that end in publication no matter the author order,
the number of citations or the type of journal where publications appear.
Policies for authorship should also address the common assumption that fee-for-service precludes the need for credit in the form of co
authorship. Authorship status is not negated by the payment of fees necessary for a consulting team to operate (LeBlanc et al., 2022). Further
more, team members in positions that are not evaluated based on publications should nevertheless be credited as co-authors using the same
criteria. Forgoing authorship because it is not important for a current position's evaluation can disadvantage people later in their careers. In addi
tion to equity, co-authorship may be important for that individual's professional development should they want to switch institutions, move into a
new role in the future or be part of a grant application, they may find themselves in a position where a publication history is expected.
While authorship in academic publications is an important form of credit, it is not the only one. Team leaders should acknowledge the work
of individuals in their team through other mechanisms. Some examples may include posting highlights of their work on a website or newsletter,
bringing their team member's work to the attention of administrators or suggesting other outlets to disseminate their work. Giving credit means
attributing the accomplishments of your team members to them by name, especially to other influential leaders within the institution and the pro
fession. Instead of talking about how successful a specific program has been, team leaders should talk about the success of the named team mem
bers collaborating on the program.
Not all credit or recognition needs to come from team leaders or management. Supporting an inclusive environment also means finding ways
for team members to recognize and give credit to each other for good work and helping each other. For example, team members can write “high
fives” to acknowledge their peers, either on online platforms or by posting notes in a shared office space. This practice lets team members recog
nize things they value that might go unnoticed by the team leader and helps reinforce team values such as collaboration.
4 | MAKE TACIT KNOWLEDGE EXPLICIT
Documenting and communicating resources and processes leads to a more inclusive and cohesive team
Tacit knowledge is the knowledge gained through experience, usually over long periods of time, and is generally difficult to articulate and
share (Polanyi, 1967). Experiences shape the acquisition of tacit knowledge, and variation in these experiences leads to a unique base of knowl
edge for each team member to draw from. For example, Gardner and Holley (2011) describe challenges that first-generation doctoral students
face due to a dearth of tacit knowledge necessary for navigating the academic environment. Surveyed students expressed hardships associated
with not knowing which questions to ask and where to seek help. In another example, Carson et al. (2019) describe how early-career faculty from
historically underrepresented groups lack the tacit knowledge to navigate service requirements. For example, a statistics or data science consult
ing team member without an advanced degree may lack the tacit knowledge of academic hierarchies other team members gained through their
graduate school experience. Conversely, team members entering the workforce and joining a consulting team straight from their doctoral program
may not understand performance review and promotion processes. This “hidden knowledge” can place employees at a disadvantage and have
negative implications for retention and functional group dynamics.
For members of data science and statistical consulting teams, this tacit knowledge is most pertinent to understanding how the organization
operates. That is, resource navigation, communication norms and standard processes are often implied, and new recruits are expected to “pick
things up on their own.” To foster an inclusive team, intentional efforts should be made to transfer such tacit knowledge to all team members. In
addition to creating inclusivity, this practice can lead to shared learning within the team and improved deliverables and experiences for
collaborators.
One approach to foster an equitable working environment in data science and statistical consulting teams is to share knowledge intentionally
and transparently, where expectations, processes and evaluation metrics are clearly communicated. Providing team members with explicit
