5 | ESTABLISH CLEAR PERFORMANCE REVIEW PROCESSES
Clear and explicit expectations and performance review processes enable mutual understanding to the benefit of staff and team leaders
Credit for work and making tacit knowledge explicit become a critical issue when conducting evaluations either through scheduled periodic
performance reviews or promotion review processes. Regardless of a data scientist's or statistician's position, the performance review process
should provide an opportunity to recognize the impactful contributions that the data scientist and statistician have made for the team, the
department and the university (Griffith et al., 2022; Mazumdar et al., 2015). For a team leader, this process is an opportunity to motivate team
members, understand their perspectives and needs and recognize achievements. Well-designed, clear and thoughtfully considered evaluation
criteria and performance review processes can lead to improved employee engagement, trust between team members and leaders and reduce
inequities and unconscious biases for the benefit of overall organizational performance and employee retention (Posselt, 2020, p. 154; Culture
Amp, 2023).
Regardless of the individual roles (i.e., staff, faculty) and contract type (i.e, temporary, tenure-track), a transparent performance review process
starts by setting defined goals with the team member and clear, corresponding assessment criteria for the team leader to apply. SMART (Specific,
Measurable, Achievable, Relevant and Time-bound) goals are one framework that can be used for aligning goals with evaluation criteria
(Doran, 1981). Whatever framework is used, both team leaders and team members must have a mutual understanding of the goals and evaluation
criteria without ambiguity. For example, a goal or promotion criteria requiring that an individual demonstrate that they are a recognized scholar
by publishing in peer-reviewed journals is not sufficient if what is really meant is that the candidate must be the first (or sole) author on a methods
development or theoretical statistics manuscript in a top-tier statistics journal. While the appropriateness of this criteria in the modern era of sta
tistics and data science is debatable, if it is a performance requirement, the criteria could explicitly state that the individual should publish 1–3
peer-reviewed journal articles with at least one in a journal from an explicitly named list. As another example, if a data scientist or statistical con
sultant's job responsibilities specify 50% effort for consultation activities, including hourly meetings, data analysis and grant support, a clear per
formance review process should indicate whether the consultant has discretion as to which activities they focus on (e.g. allocating all of this effort
to consultation meetings and none to grant support) or whether there are expectations for them to meet specific goals in each area (e.g., 4–5
hourly meetings per week). Clearly specifying these criteria will help to ensure that the team member is not spending more of their time in any
one, or all, of these components than expected. That is, it is easy to become wholly invested in one component and not meet the needs of the
other criteria, or to become burnt out trying to do it all.
Once expectations are set, it is important to track progress. Rather than a one-time, annual meeting to evaluate progress, it may be beneficial
for both the team leader and members to schedule periodic meetings to assess and discuss progress towards goals. A bi-directional communica
tion channel is a productive means for building trust and ensuring that the team member has the skills and tools to successfully fulfil their role. An
on-going evaluation process helps mitigate problems when they arise and prevent unexpected and possibly unpleasant feedback at a later point.
A method that can be employed to provide feedback on performance or to share feedback with the next level of leadership is the STAR
(Situation, Task, Action and Result) approach (Development Dimensions International, n.d.). While traditionally used for structuring interview
responses, the team leader can use this approach to detail performance examples (situation), describe responsibilities in those examples (task),
identify activities relevant to the examples (action) and report on outcomes (result). This format can be used for positive feedback, specifying what
was done well or when needed, developmental feedback, which describes a more effective approach, can be provided.
Whereas, for example, the promotion criteria and the eligibility between the faculty levels of Assistant to Associate Professor may be clearly
defined, they may be more opaque for promotion for other job roles (e.g., Data Scientist to Senior Data Scientist or Associate to Full Professor).
Thus, establishing clear and transparent evaluation criteria is especially important for these team members. For instance, consider the criteria for
advancing from a Data Scientist to a Senior Data Scientist. A Senior Data Scientist may be expected to successfully scope and manage projects.
The team leader can provide a listing of potential evidence that the Data Scientist can provide to demonstrate they will be successful at the next
level (e.g., the type and number of projects that they have contributed to the development of scopes of work).
