6 | CHAMPION CAREER DEVELOPMENT
Help team members identify and navigate career pathways to retain diverse talent
Data science and statistics are constantly evolving fields. For team members to maintain their knowledge and expertise, ongoing technical
learning and training is required. Supporting career development begins by allocating time and resources for team members to engage in this
foundational professional growth and learning.
However, championing career development requires more than sending team members to conferences or classes. Team leaders should help
team members think about their whole career trajectory – possibly beyond their current position or organization. This forward-thinking necessi
tates honest and clear evaluations and discussions of a team member's current skillset, the skills needed for the next step in their career ladder,
the skills that they would like to develop and avenues for developing the skills (see Van Tuyl, 2023, for examples of academic data scientist career
ladders). Team leaders can help identify those avenues by mapping current work and accomplishments to skills team members are looking to
develop or need for their next career stage. While team leaders can help, ultimately, team members need to take ownership of their own career
development. Two steps team leaders can encourage team members to take are connecting with others who are already in the types of positions
they would like to move into and looking at the skills required in job postings of positions that interest them. Once there is a joint vision of the
skills and experience team members would like to achieve, team leaders can seek opportunities for team members to engage in that type of work.
By seeking out opportunities that align with their team members' goals and using their position to advocate for their team members' ability to
take on new challenges, team leaders can become a ‘sponsor’ and not just a ‘mentor’ (Hewlett, 2013). A sponsor is a professional champion that
“guides others toward [the sponsee's] work and contributions” (Ensor, 2015) and goes beyond advice to support and advocacy, “connecting [the
sponsee] to important players and assignments” (Hewlett, 2013). Sponsorship has been shown to improve retention and advancement for individ
uals from underrepresented groups (Hewlett & Ihezie, 2022).
Sponsorship ideally involves identifying significant opportunities for team members to stretch their abilities and advance their careers. How
ever, team leaders should also provide more routine pathways (e.g., time, resources) for individuals to participate in activities and create deliver
ables needed for advancement. For example, in a data science collaboration context, suppose that a key evaluation criteria for an individual is to
present a collaborative research project at a conference. The team leader can work with the team members on time allocation (e.g., adjusting time
spent on other projects as necessary) and can provide resources (e.g., funding) to present the work. Less directly, team members can present their
work regularly within the group to support individual presentation and communication skills.
7 | EMPOWER TEAM MEMBERS TO WORK AUTONOMOUSLY
Fostering independence communicates the value of team members' work and supports a culture of contribution
Empowering team members and fostering autonomy and independence in employees is crucial for promoting creativity, innovation and per
sonal growth, as well as the retention of talent (Cagan & Jones, 2020). Empowerment means creating an environment where people have owner
ship over the outcomes of their work rather than only the tasks (Cagan & Jones, 2020). Team leaders can value, reward and celebrate
independent thinking and initiative to reinforce the autonomy and independence of all team members.
An important aspect of empowering team members is the delegation of authority to give people ownership of their work. Delegation allows
decisions to be made by those with subject expertise and understanding of the issues, who are most likely to be the first to recognize appropriate
solutions (Bloom, 2020). When delegating, a team leader must be willing to take the risk associated with letting someone else be responsible for a
project, trust team members to successfully complete the task and be patient while they may be learning new skills (Nelson, 1993; see, for exam
ple, the “independent R&D” of Nowviskie, 2013). For the delegation to be successful, it is imperative that team leaders clearly communicate
expectations, goals and the details of authority granted for the completion of the project. Team leaders can then adopt a coaching approach
(i.e., working alongside people rather than just guiding them) and help team members utilize their potential without asserting control over the pro
ject (i.e., micromanaging) or taking credit for their accomplishments (Cagan & Jones, 2020). These approaches require active work from the team
leader to assess their team, develop coaching plans and offer regular feedback and guidance. As a part of coaching, the team leader can share their
