8 | LEARN FROM DIVERSE EXPERIENCES
Create space and opportunities for team members to share how they experience the world and how that affects their work
Facilitating conversations with and among team members is important for reaping the benefits of a diverse team and creating a culture that is
inclusive of varied experiences. As Rock et al. (2016) put it, “capitalizing on diversity means highlighting — not hiding from — differences.” Simi
larly, White's (2022) suggestions for leaders looking to build an inclusive organizational culture start with “actively listening and learning” about
the experiences of team members. Yet 61% of employees report “covering,” or taking steps to avoid drawing attention to, at least one aspect of
their identity that may have a stigma associated with while at work, even in workplaces with stated commitments to inclusion (Smith &
Yoshino, 2019).
Successfully navigating complex conversations about personal differences requires building shared values and trust within a team. Pérez
(2021) outlines steps to build trust through conversation and help a team progress to having transformative conversations in emotionally intelli
gent ways: 1) asking discovery questions, 2) actively listening, 3) using dramatic emphasis and 4) acknowledging life milestones and work achieve
ments. Su (2019) suggests steps for building trust and psychological safety in a team, including modelling healthy conflict, zero tolerance for
bullying and developing a culture of appreciation. It is critical to address bullying and poor interpersonal behaviours with care and immediately, as
team members' work and development can be significantly impeded if not.
Including someone as part of a team means listening to and learning from their story. Each team member has a unique history, identity and
personality that will affect their experience within the team, in their consultations and within the institution. These personal identities and histo
ries (e.g., age, gender, race and more) will affect their experience. For data science and statistics consulting teams, there are also varied experi
ences in when and how people learned to code, familiarity with different research domains, level of degree, role at the university (faculty, staff,
student) and job title. Beyond their backgrounds, team members will also have differences in how they experience ongoing events, such as
changes in the work environment and broader news and political events.
When learning about the experiences of others, avoid either reducing their experience to one aspect of their identity or ignoring the systemic
factors that may contribute to their experience. Team leaders can also listen to how team members frame their experiences and share their own
understanding rather than interpreting it for them. For example, both responding to a particular woman's experience with discrimination by saying
it's something all women face and saying that discrimination is something many people of different identities face can be dismissive of someone's
individual experience. The former may deny the particular ways an individual's multiple identities of race, sexuality and age intersect with gender
in their experience. The latter ignores the specific systemic factors that disadvantage women, which can undermine efforts to address them.
With data science and statistics consulting teams, learning from diverse experiences should also extend to exploring how collaborators inter
acting with the team may have different experiences. For example, raising awareness of how a first-generation undergraduate student's experi
ence consulting the team may differ from that of an early career faculty member with a history of qualitative research will help team members be
more effective in their collaborations. Being intentional and direct in discussing how individuals' differences affect their experience working with
the team will also help team members reach out to new collaborators in ways that speak to their needs and experiences.
9 | SUPPORT TEAM MEMBERS IN NAVIGATING POWER DYNAMICS
Position, identity, situation and expertise can all affect the power dynamics between those involved in collaboration and contribute to or
hamper the success of the interaction
Identifying and navigating power dynamics is necessary on both sides of a consultant/client relationship. As with learning from diverse experi
ences, acknowledging and explicitly having conversations about the factors that shape academic consulting relationships is critical for addressing
issues that can inhibit an inclusive environment.
The power relations or dynamics (see Foucault, 1980; Stainback et al., 2010) between a consultant and a client will be affected by
• Position: the role in the organization that both people occupy (e.g., undergraduate student, graduate student, staff, faculty, IT support,
researcher staff).
• Identity: the personal characteristics and identities of each, including age, gender, ethnicity/race, abilities/disabilities, etc.
