Position is mostly a function of the institution and system that people are operating in and is not easily changed. Supporting team members on this
dimension starts with making any tacit knowledge about assumed status differences between role types explicit and acknowledging it. For exam
ple, acknowledging that faculty consultants on a team may be less likely than staff consultants to need to advocate for their role as a co-author
on a paper. Positional power differentials can be addressed by adopting practices and norms for the team that attempt to adjust the dynamic. For
example, the team can use first names as the default form of address for both team members and clients. Consulting appointments could be
scheduled in the consulting team's space rather than a consultant being expected to travel to a faculty member's office. Team members can
include academic credentials or positions in their email signatures.
While identity is also based on characteristics that are largely fixed, the ways in which individuals' identities interact depend upon cultural and
social context. For example, being Sikh in India carries with it a complex cultural and political context of which people in the US may be completely
unaware. In the multicultural environment of a university or research institution, people bring their cultural and social backgrounds, including their
expectations for interacting with people of different identities and assumptions about power dynamics. Supporting team members in educating
themselves about such differences through professional development funding or goals is one way to improve their ability to navigate these
dynamics.
Power dynamics that result from the situation are the ones that can most easily be influenced by the team leader. As Pozzebon and
Pinsonneault (2012) found, the initial set-up of a project is particularly important. Having clear in-take or collaboration initiation processes that
reference written policies can help to set expectations up front and frame the interaction. Written policies also make it easier for consultants to
address breaches in the expectations or scope of the relationship by providing something they can reference. For example, written project
agreements that clearly outline the scope of the engagement can help consultants address requests that would expand the scope – such as inves
tigating new questions or engaging in data cleaning tasks that were not part of the initial agreement.
Power differentials resulting from expertise may help a consultant who may otherwise feel they are in a weaker or vulnerable position in a
consulting engagement. However, a consultant needs to be confident in their expertise and see themselves as an expert for their expertise to play
an equalizing role in a relationship, as well as being comfortable in unknown and new areas. Consultants who have received training are anecdot
ally more likely to develop this confidence (Sharp et al., 2021). Similarly, a student seeking data science assistance from a faculty member may gain
confidence when reminded that they are the experts in their own research domain and project. Asking someone seeking assistance to describe
their research or provide input on how they have seen a problem addressed in other research in their field can help them access and share this
expertise, leading them to be more comfortable sharing other areas where they need assistance.
Helping consultants recognize these dynamics and explicitly discussing strategies for addressing them, both individually and as a team, can
help the team have the language and a framework for discussing issues that may arise in their consulting engagements. Sharp et al. (2021) devel
oped 10 statistical collaboration training videos and scripts along with discussion questions as a resource for facilitating these discussions.
10 | HAVE THE DIFFICULT CONVERSATIONS
Advocating for the team requires sometimes instigating and engaging in uncomfortable or difficult conversations with team members, collabo
rators or unit leadership
Being an inclusive leader requires courage (Bourke, 2016). While this may come in many forms, one place courage is required is having con
versations that address when a team member, collaborator or senior leader is failing to meet your team's expectations for an inclusive
environment.
With team members, this can mean enforcing zero-tolerance policies for bullying, racist or sexist language or other biased or exclusionary
behaviours. This may involve challenging team members who use potentially harmful language that could be derogatory to consider the history
and implications of their word choices. It may require reminding team members that their peers or collaborators may have family members who
are affected by government policies or actions they are vocally supporting or opposing. Importantly, team leaders should recognize these behav
iours in action and acknowledge the personal experiences of those that report them. Specific to data science or statistics projects, the team leader
may need to remind or educate team members of the complex history and implications of data encoding choices around race, ethnicity, citizen
ship, gender and other personal characteristics. It takes courage to directly and clearly address such situations, but doing so is essential for setting
clear expectations for the team.
With collaborators, team leaders need to proactively ask about team members' interactions with constituents or clients and be prepared to
engage in conversations with those collaborators to address any issues. For example, a data scientist working with a faculty member who repeat
edly uses inappropriate language may be hesitant to bring this up, let alone address it themselves, especially given the power differentials
