team members should come to the team leader if something like this occurs, and that the team leader will take action if team members do report
it. Unless these expectations are explicitly stated or team members observe the team leader engage in such conversations, team members may
assume they have to deal with such situations on their own.
Team leaders may also need to have difficult conversations with their own managers or stakeholders. As with team members or collaborators,
team leaders may need to address behaviour by senior leaders that hinders the inclusive environment they are working to lead. However, difficult
conversations may also extend to ways in which the systems or policies in place are failing to support the inclusive environment the team leader
is working hard to build and support. For example, units that recruit job applicants through personal networks instead of open job postings, use
exclusionary language in job descriptions, exclude applicants based on poor “culture fit” or discount applicants that have any gaps in their work
history are undermining efforts to support an inclusive environment from the start. Conversations to change such practices are likely to be
difficult, but having them is critical.
Difficult conversations do not need to be punitive. Aggressive or harmful behaviours or words may not be intentional, but left unaddressed,
harmful dynamics are likely to continue. Approaching difficult conversations collaboratively can help everyone move towards more productive
and inclusive relationships. The goals are to set clear expectations, establish that people will be held to the established standards and create an
environment for improved collaboration moving forward. See Patterson et al. (2002) and Hancher (2022) for tips on having these difficult, but
critical, conversations.
11 | DEVELOP MANAGEMENT SKILLS
Developing skills to consistently and equitably implement best management practices is a prerequisite for creating an inclusive environment
Diverse teams have many benefits, but they also create challenges that must be managed for the benefits to be realized (Rock et al., 2016).
The practices discussed in earlier sections become easier to enact if team leaders have good fundamental management practices in place that help
establish positive and trusting relationships with team members.
In our experience, data science or statistical consultant team leaders, like other academic leaders, rarely receive training in how to be an emo
tionally intelligent team leader as part of their academic education or career. Fortunately, there are many good resources on how to build these
crucial skills, likely including training courses and programs offered by their institutions. Seeking out and participating in such training is not a nice
to-have or bonus activity – it is core to the work of being a team leader and needs to be prioritized as such. From email newsletters to books, from
podcasts to online courses to multi-day training, there are multiple ways to engage in continuous learning (see Table 1 for some places to start).
Foundational management practices include: holding regular one-on-one meetings with members of your team - including students, providing
both positive and constructive feedback on a regular basis, establishing regular methods and practices for communicating with the team and hav
ing explicit discussions about job performance expectations. Performing management tasks well requires building trust with the team and
employing emotional intelligence, both of which can be built through specific practice (Pérez, 2021; Sebre et al., 2021).
Managing up is also a crucial skill (Rousmaniere, 2015). Supporting the statistics and data science team through the practices above will only
go so far if the next-level team leader, department chair or stakeholders are not supportive partners in the process. The statistics and data science
