12 | CONCLUSION
This manuscript provides ideas for ways in which team leaders can do good and lead an inclusive statistics or data science consulting team
(Table 2). Creating an inclusive and collaborative working environment may be time-consuming initially. However, this should be considered a
T A B L E 2 Summary of leadership and management practices discussed in this work.
Practice Description Examples
Ensure people get credit
Ensuring that team members receive appropriate credit for their work will support future advancement opportunities.
Advocate for authorship on publications. Publicly recognize team members' contributions regardless of authorship status. Provide opportunities for team members to recognize each other's work and contributions to the team.
Make tacit knowledge explicit
Documenting and communicating resources and processes leads to a more inclusive and cohesive team.
Document workflows and standard operating procedures within the team. Create mentoring opportunities. Internally share resources and tools, such as documentation on common methods (e.g., mixed effects models), tips for using popular tools (e.g., ggplot2) and templates.
Establish clear performance review processes
Clear and explicit expectations and performance review processes enable mutual understanding to the benefit of staff and team leaders.
Put evaluation criteria in writing. Discuss evaluation criteria with team members to ensure shared understanding. Regularly discuss performance progress and challenges with team members.
Champion career development
Help team members identify and navigate career pathways to retain diverse talent.
Discuss professional aspirations with team members. Help team members identify connections between their work and the skills needed for the next step in their career. Sponsor team members in pursuing activities and opportunities that will grow their experience and skills.
Empower team members to work autonomously
Fostering independence communicates the value of team members' work and supports a culture of contribution.
Delegate responsibility. Coach team members, do not micromanage.
Learn from diverse experiences
Create space and opportunities for team members to share how they experience the world and how that affects their work.
Listen. Avoid interpreting other people's experiences and identities for them. Consider the experiences of both team members and those collaborating with the team.
Support team members in navigating power dynamics
Position, identity, situation and expertise can all affect the power dynamics between those involved in collaboration and contribute to or hamper the success of the interaction.
Explicitly discuss strategies for navigating status differences, such as norms around the use of titles and being explicit about a project's scope. Support team members in learning about ways different identities may influence people's experiences.
Have the difficult conversations
Advocating for the team requires sometimes instigating and engaging in uncomfortable or difficult conversations with team members, collaborators or unit leadership.
Address inappropriate language by collaborators, especially when directed at team members. “Manage up” to change exclusionary language in job descriptions.
Develop management skills
Developing skills to consistently and equitably implement best management practices is a prerequisite for creating an inclusive environment.
Pursue training in leadership and management skills. Hold one-on-one meetings with all team members regularly.
